    {
      "file": "src/Alert.h",
      "description": "This is the model of the alert, this is what we'll be iterating on.\r\n\r\n\r\nWe'll make aggregations on alerts and create functions to process alerts.\r\n\r\nTake a few seconds and take a look at it. ",
      "line": 6,
      "title": "Get to know your data!"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Program.cpp",
      "description": "Let's mock some alerts and get started!\r\n\r\nWe'll take a look at GHCP Completions, Inline chat (and comments) and finally Chat in that order.\r\n\r\nJump right in by navigating to the relevant file where the questions are according the the methods below.",
      "line": 7,
      "title": "Mocks and order of exercises"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Using GHCP completions is optimal for Short, Specific and Simple logic (the 3 `S` principle), remember that!\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFor best completions results - \r\nName a variable according to the logic you expect and then refactor the name after.\r\n\r\nPro tips:\r\n1. GHCP also suggests names (!) if you press Refactor (F2) on the function/variable name.\r\n2. Watch this for more context on the [3 `S` Principal](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hh1nOX14TyY&t=38s&ab_channel=VisualStudioCode)",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completions introduction"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Let's start easy, we'll create a variable to store the total number of alerts.\r\n\r\nSimply place your caret after the equal sign and the `Space` key",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completion #1"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Create a function that receives a location name and finds the alerts which happened with that `locationName`.\r\n\r\nBut what about the return type? Please use `auto` for all return types, we will get to it later using inline chat.",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completion #2"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Create a function that returns the aggregated count of alerts by Severity, example output: {\"Critical\": 2, \"Medium\": 1, ....}\r\n\r\n\r\nFriendly reminder that occasionally, you may not get the completion you want, you may use \r\n\r\n`Ctrl + Enter`\r\n\r\nTo open the `Suggestions panel`, and see several options (it may present very bad options, so proceed with care and feel free to only take what you need and ignore the rest)",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completion #3"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Create a function to get all alerts which happened between two specific dates.\r\n\r\nAnother thought, how would that change if you wanted to get alerts between specific hours?",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completion #4"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Top Alert Sources: Create a function to identify the most common source of alerts. ",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completion #5"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "Let's try to be more specific, when you type `alertsWithLongName` what is the length you get (try it)? Does that satisfy you? What if you wanted it to be a little longer let's say with 12 characters? \r\nThink of how'd you write name that variable in a more specific way - you can always rename after.",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completions #6"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Completions.cpp",
      "description": "So we now know to name the variable according to what the logic we want it to execute, and then rename it for easier reading. \r\n\r\n\r\nAs a perfect segway for inline chat, let's now fix all these \"auto\" return types.\r\nSelect all the methods which include the auto return type, open inline chat (using CTRL + I) and use the following prompt: \r\n\r\n\"**Instead of auto, use correct return type**\"\r\n\r\nGithub Copilot should fix these to be the correct return type auto-magically ✨.",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Completions summary"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/InlineChat.cpp",
      "description": "Using GHCP Inline Chat fits scenarios where you need more than one thing at a time, a very specific logic that's too long for a variable name, or you want to provide examples (yes, that's a recommendation!).\r\n\r\n\r\nIn VS Code the default shortcut is ctrl + I to open inline chat",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Inline chat introduction"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/InlineChat.cpp",
      "description": "Your task: \r\n\r\nCreate a function which takes an alert and checks if the `AlertStatus` is not `Resolved` for more than seven days, log to console if so.\r\n\r\nNow you're probably thinking - \r\n\r\nCan I copy and paste this? The answer is yes. But how will you practice phrasing your requirements? \r\n\r\nDoes the below code work? If not, try selecting the function, and prompt inline chat again with `\"/fix\"`.\r\n\rTip: you may want to add the codebase as a context to the prompt. You can do that by clicking the attach button and selecting codebase or just adding #codebase\r\n\r\nLazy tip:\r\nIf the thought of typing a lot is what deters you, consider talking to VS Code! Using the extension \"VS Code Speech\" (Also recommended in workspace and should be installed) you can simply talk to VS Code with speech to text. This works very well and can be done both in editor, in inline chat as well as in the chat window, give it a go!\r\nOnce installed, you may invoke it within the inline chat using another click of `Ctrl + I` or simply press the mic button",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Inline chat #1"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/InlineChat.cpp",
      "description": "Create a method which accepts a list of alerts as a parameter, checks if there are any alerts with Critical severity which are still active, return these alerts.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nNow we have to stop for a second to ask:\r\nBut why can't I simply use comments instead? They are also left in the repo explaining what the code below does! \r\n\r\n1. Code gets refactored, comments usually do not. These comments will demand a cleanup in the future and get disregarded and become unrelevant.\r\n2. More importantly, comments with GHCP aren't iterable, meaning it's a one-shot completions attempt. If you get it wrong, you have to edit your comment and try again. When using the inline chat you can then reply with what needs to be fixed. Just think of how rare it is when you code things that work for the first time, the better you are explaining what you want to GHCP, the better GHCP will help you, sometimes it takes a few rounds of explanations, a.k.a. \"inline chat ;)\"",
      "line": 4,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 162
        }
      },
      "title": "Inline chat #2"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/InlineChat.cpp",
      "description": "Create a method which receives a location and a severity level and checks if location had any alerts with provided severity",
      "line": 4,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 124
        }
      },
      "title": "Inline chat #3"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/InlineChat.cpp",
      "description": "Filter alerts which have a source of everything but words ending with 'phone'.\r\ni.e., Source == 'Sensor', yes, 'telephone', no, 'microphone', no. Log to console when matched and return alerts",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "Inline chat #4"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "Using GHCP Chat is ideal for discussions, questions and guidance and reassurance of your code. \r\nWith GCHP chat (CTRL + ALT + I), it's much easier to provide more context using ALIASES, we'll talk about aliases next.\r\nThe more context you add, the more capable Copilot would be.\r\n\r\n\r\nSome (none?) standard examples one can use:\r\n1. \"Check if I missed any edge cases in #selection\"\r\n2. \"Can you provide a numerical example?\" (If you can't remember if you should compare time)\r\n3. \"How do I run the project?\" More often than not, the instructions may be a little obscure, Copilot knows how to simplify and provide simple commands.\r\n4. Using it for some git operations! \"What command to edit the last commit message?\"\r\n\r\nRemember context is everything, if GHCP chat starts providing bad information, it may be confused with older contexts, don't ask him about C# and jump to typescript, create a new chat for these subject jumps. You can always navigate to previous sessions.\r\n",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat introduction"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "Let's say you remembered there's an enum for `AlarmSeverity`, but looking at the file structure, you can't recall what it is.\r\nYou may use the @workspace alias or the #codebase context accessor in GHCP to search for it. Try both, first use @workspace, then in chat, click up arrow and replace @workspace with #codebase. \r\n\r\nThese two GHCP functions iterate over *all the project* and can answer your questions on a project scope.\r\n\r\nLet's start by asking it where you can find the AlarmSeverity enum? \r\n\r\nUse the @workspace alias (in the beginning of the question) or #codebase anywhere in your question, there's a subtle difference by how it displays the answer, give it a go!\r\n\r\nTip: When searching in the project scope, be sure to deselect the active file attached by clicking the eye icon next to it.",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat #1"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "Let's give it another go. \r\nIn the repo, I've hidden a few SECRETs. Try using Copilot to find these secrets",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat #2"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "Now let's use #selection, this is what you should be using when asking code-specific-questions or writing unit tests. Highlight the text desired and in your question (even in inline-chat) use the #selection alias.\r\n\r\nLet's try it now: \r\n1. Highlight the method\r\n2. Ask in chat \"What edge cases did I miss\"?\r\n",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat #3"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "While trivial, lots of developers tend to jump into code and start reading it to understand, natural language is still faster to comprehend. You can use the `/explain` alias and read it in plain text, it's pretty accurate.",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat #4"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "Finally, let's write some unit tests using GHCP.\r\nRemember what we said? \r\nHighlight the desired code to test, in our case the Divider function. let's write up unittests for it, remember to fix the missing edge case first.\r\n\r\nIn chat, write:\r\n\r\n`@workspace /tests Write unit tests for #selection using NUnit`\r\n\r\n\r\nDon't forget to metion which test framework you're using as it may vary.",
      "line": 3,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 6,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 6,
          "character": 54
        }
      },
      "title": "Chat #5"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Chat.h",
      "description": "There are many more aliases, even for the `Terminal` which is extremely useful to debug errors, recall git commands much more. I urge you to go into the Chat input and...\r\n1. Start by typing \"/\", browse through the list of options. These are all commands \r\n2. Delete the \"/\" and try \"@\". These are all context \r\n3. Finally, delete the \"@\" and take a look at the # commands.\r\n\r\nThere's a lot the Chat experience can provide and teach, don't hesitate to explore!\r\nUse the up and down arrows and spend a little time to read the descriptions of these commands.",
      "line": 3,
      "title": "Chat #6"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Alert.h",
      "description": "**Copilot Edits** (Shortcut: _Ctrl+Shift+I_)\r\n\r\nUsed to **edit multiple files at the same time**! \r\n\r\nChanges are applied directly to the files provided as context / detected by Copilot, these files are also called \"working set\" and recently the 10 file limit has been removed (yay).\r\n\r\nLet's start with the prompt:\r\n\r\n\"_Add a logger to the alert class and log the important properties_\"",
      "line": 26,
      "title": "Copilot edits 😮"
    },
    {
      "file": "src/Program.cpp",
      "description": "Introducing Agent mode, same functionality of editing multiple files, but with iterations. Let's call it edits ++ \r\n\r\nLet's ask Copilot to create the spec for our functionality:\r\n\r\n`\r\nI want the main function to use the different functions that exists in the code base. And allow the user to filter specific alerts based on inputs. So this will now be a console app that runs on the generated alerts, and while the alerts are generated every second in the background. Without interfering with alert generation, the user will be able to get the current status by choosing a number from the menu which will execute a specific function.`\r\n\r\nBut before that, **copy the prompt above** and let's use our [special prompt](.github\\prompts\\Feature breakdown prompt.prompt.md):\r\n\r\n\r\nCtrl + Shift + P -> Use prompt\r\n\r\nPaste the prompt, and take the response.",
      "line": 18,
      "title": "Github Copilot Agent 🤯"
    },
    {
      "file": "../cpp/mcp_server/alert_mcp_server.cpp",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "🆕 MCP: Give Copilot Live Tool Access",
      "description": "# 🆕 Model Context Protocol (MCP) — Copilot with Superpowers\r\n\r\n**MCP** is a standardized protocol that lets Copilot call your own tools and APIs — not just read files, but *act* on live data. Think of it as USB-C for AI tools.\r\n\r\n## What You're Looking At\r\n\r\nThis is a C++ **MCP server** implementing JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio — the raw protocol that all MCP SDKs abstract away. Great for understanding what's happening under the hood!\r\n\r\nIt exposes 3 tools:\r\n- `get_alerts(severity?)` — query the in-memory alert store\r\n- `get_alert_stats()` — count by severity\r\n- `create_alert(title, severity)` — add a new alert\r\n\r\n## Transport Choices\r\n\r\n| Transport | Best for |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Stdio** ⭐ | Local dev — VS Code spawns the server as a child process, zero config |\r\n| **HTTP Streamable** | Team/remote servers, production |\r\n| **SSE** *(legacy)* | Backward compatibility |\r\n\r\nC++ uses **Stdio** only (no official C++ MCP SDK yet — this implements the protocol directly).\r\n\r\n## 🎮 Try It\r\n\r\n1. Run the prompt: `Ctrl+Shift+P` → *Use Prompt* → `/MCP-Server-Builder`\r\n2. Follow the guided setup (includes compile instructions + nlohmann/json install).\r\n3. Then ask Copilot (Agent mode):\r\n```\r\nUsing the alert MCP tools, show me all critical alerts\r\nand suggest a remediation priority plan.\r\n```\r\n\r\n> **Pro Tip**: First run shows a tool-approval dialog — click *Allow*. You always control what Copilot can touch."
    },
    {
      "title": "Chat Mode Handoffs: Orchestrating Multi-Step Workflows",
      "description": "Let's set up our own custom GitHub Copilot Chat Mode to accelerate creation of Product Requirements Documents (PRDs).\n\n**Goal**: Create a mode named **PRD builder** that loads the persona instructions so you can quickly draft structured PRDs from short prompts.\n\n### Steps\n1. Open the Copilot Chat panel (Ctrl + Alt + I).\n1. Click the mode dropdown (where you pick *Ask* / *Agent*). You probably noticed there are already two custom modes, let's create our own by selecting **Configure modes** (see screenshot below).\n\n    ![Configure Modes](./assets/ConfigureModes.png)\n\n1. In the configuration UI, click **Create a new custom mode**.\n\n    ![Create Mode](./assets/Create a new custom mode.png)\n\n1. Let's use the User level scope to share this across projects instead of being project specific\n\n    ![User Data Folder](./assets/UserDataFolder.png)    \n        \n1. Name it exactly: `PRD builder`.\n1. Open this file `PRD-Builder-Persona.md` ([link](./personas/PRD-Builder-Persona.md)) and copy its entire contents into the new mode's definition/instructions text area.\n1. (Optional) Pick an emoji/icon to make it stand out (🧱, 📄, 🚀).\n1. Save the mode.\n\nCongratulations, you've created a custom mode.\n\n\n### What This Gives You\n- Consistent PRD structure (intro, goals, user stories, requirements, non-goals, metrics)\n- Automatic reminder to ask clarifying questions first\n- Faster onboarding for teammates who can reuse the same mode\n\n### Pro Tips\n- You can store multiple personas (e.g., *Test Plan Builder*, *API Spec Writer*) the same way.\n- Version your persona files in Git so the team can improve them collaboratively.\n- If you update the persona text here, re-open the mode config and re-paste to sync.\n\nWhen you're done, try giving it a *very short* feature idea and see how it first asks you clarifying questions (that's by design!).\n\nGive it a spin—your future specs will thank you. ✨"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Plan the Alert System with /plan",
      "description": "# 🆕 The /plan Agent (v1.109)\r\n\r\nOpen Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`) and type:\r\n\r\n```\r\n/plan I want to build a full alert management system with 3 components: an alert generator that creates randomized Alert structs, a classifier that categorizes them by severity and location, and a console UI for viewing and filtering alerts.\r\n```\r\n\r\nWatch the 4-phase workflow:\r\n1. **🔍 Discovery** – Analyzes your codebase and requirements\r\n2. **🤝 Alignment** – Asks clarifying questions before committing\r\n3. **📐 Design** – Proposes architecture and file structure\r\n4. **✨ Refinement** – Iterates until you approve\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: The agent won't start coding until alignment is complete — this prevents costly rework! 🎯"
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/skills/generate-alerts/SKILL.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "🆕 Agent Skills: /generate-alerts",
      "description": "# 🆕 Agent Skills (GA in v1.109)\r\n\r\nYou're looking at a **SKILL.md** file — a reusable AI command.\r\n\r\nNotice the structure:\r\n1. **YAML Frontmatter** – name, description\r\n2. **Markdown Body** – Instructions Copilot follows\r\n\r\nSkills are loaded **on-demand** — Copilot only reads the full body when your request matches.\r\n\r\n## Try It\r\n\r\nOpen Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`) and type:\r\n```\r\n/generate-alerts 10\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe same skill works in VS Code, Copilot CLI, and coding agents — it's an open standard ([agentskills.io](https://agentskills.io)).\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Build a library of skills for your team: `/run-tests`, `/deploy-staging`, `/generate-docs` 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Bootstrap Instructions with /init",
      "description": "# 🆕 /init Command (v1.109)\r\n\r\n1. Open Chat (`Ctrl+Alt+I`)\r\n2. Type: `/init`\r\n\r\nCopilot will:\r\n🔍 Analyze your project structure (detects C++, headers, build system)\r\n📚 Discover existing AI conventions (AGENTS.md, skills)\r\n📝 Generate `copilot-instructions.md` with coding standards, build commands, and best practices\r\n\r\n✅ Great for onboarding and priming Copilot for your project.\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Run `/init` after major architectural changes. Commit the generated file so the whole team benefits! 🎯"
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/agents/alert-expert.agent.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "🆕 Build Specialized Agents",
      "description": "# 🆕 Custom Agent Control (v1.109)\r\n\r\nCheck out this agent's frontmatter:\r\n- `user-invocable: true` → Appears in agents dropdown\r\n- `disable-model-invocation: false` → Copilot can auto-select it\r\n\r\nNow open `../.github/agents/ui-builder.agent.md`:\r\n- `disable-model-invocation: true` → Manual selection only\r\n\r\nNew in v1.109:\r\n- **`agents:`** → Limit which subagents it can invoke\r\n- **`model:`** → Fallback model list: `['claude-sonnet-4', 'gpt-4']`\r\n\r\n## Try It\r\n1. Open Chat → agents dropdown → select **alert-expert**\r\n2. Ask: \"What are the classification rules for Critical vs Error severity?\"\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use `user-invocable: false` for internal agents only callable by other agents."
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/agents/orchestrator.agent.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "🆕 Wire an Agent Orchestra",
      "description": "# 🆕 Agent Orchestration (v1.109)\r\n\r\nThis is a **conductor agent** — it delegates, never implements directly.\r\n\r\nCheck the frontmatter:\r\n```yaml\r\nagents: ['alert-expert', 'ui-builder']\r\n```\r\n\r\n❌ Anti-pattern: One mega-agent that does everything\r\n✅ Best practice: Conductor + specialists\r\n\r\n## Try It\r\n1. Select **orchestrator** from agents dropdown\r\n2. Ask: \"Build the alert generator and a console UI to display alerts.\"\r\n3. Watch it delegate to alert-expert and ui-builder\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Think of orchestrators as project managers — they coordinate, not code! 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Parallel Subagents in Action",
      "description": "# 🆕 Parallel Subagents (v1.109)\r\n\r\nSubagents now run **in parallel** — tasks that took 6 minutes sequentially now take 2!\r\n\r\n## Try It\r\nAsk the orchestrator:\r\n```\r\nBuild three things simultaneously:\r\n1. Alert generator creating random Alert structs\r\n2. Severity classifier categorizing by risk level\r\n3. Console dashboard with filtering\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou'll see **multiple active spinners** — expand each to see progress, tools, and status.\r\n\r\nEach subagent has its **own context window** — no pollution of your main chat.\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use parallel subagents for independent tasks; sequential for dependent ones. 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Test Harness with Agent Hooks",
      "description": "# 🆕 Agent Hooks (v1.109)\r\n\r\nHooks run shell commands at key agent lifecycle points.\r\n\r\n## Setup\r\n1. Type `/hooks` in Chat\r\n2. Create a **Stop** hook with command: `g++ -o alert_system src/*.cpp && ./alert_system --test`\r\n\r\nNow the pattern is:\r\n1. ✍️ Write tests first\r\n2. 🤖 Let agent code autonomously\r\n3. ✅ Tests run automatically at session end\r\n4. 🔁 Agent self-corrects if tests fail\r\n\r\n## Hook Events\r\n| Event | Fires When |\r\n|-------|------------|\r\n| `SessionStart` | Session begins |\r\n| `Stop` | Session ends |\r\n| `PreToolUse` | Before any tool call |\r\n| `PostToolUse` | After any tool call |\r\n\r\nHooks work in VS Code, Claude Code, and Copilot CLI.\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use `PostToolUse` to auto-format code after edits! 🎯"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Steer the Agent Mid-Task",
      "description": "# 🆕 Message Steering (v1.109)\r\n\r\nWhile an agent response is in progress, the Send button becomes a dropdown:\r\n\r\n1️⃣ **Steer with Message** — Redirect without canceling\r\nTry: \"Add color coding: Critical=red, Error=orange, Warning=yellow, Info=blue\"\r\n\r\n2️⃣ **Add to Queue** — Send after current task completes\r\n\r\n3️⃣ **Stop and Send** — Cancel and start fresh\r\n\r\nYou can drag queued messages to reorder them!\r\n\r\n| Scenario | Action |\r\n|----------|--------|\r\n| Needs adjustment | **Steer** |\r\n| Add follow-up work | **Queue** |\r\n| Wrong direction | **Stop** |\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Use steering for refinement, queuing for sequential tasks. 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Remember Decisions with Copilot Memory",
      "description": "# 🆕 Copilot Memory (v1.109)\r\n\r\nCopilot remembers your design decisions **across sessions**!\r\n\r\n## Enable It\r\n`github.copilot.chat.copilotMemory.enabled: true`\r\n\r\n## Teach Copilot\r\n```\r\nRemember that in this project:\r\n- Critical alerts display in red\r\n- All alerts use UTC timestamps (ISO8601)\r\n- Severity distribution: 40% Info, 30% Warning, 20% Error, 10% Critical\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Test It\r\n1. Start a **new chat session**\r\n2. Ask: \"Generate 5 sample alerts\"\r\n3. 🎯 Watch Copilot apply your conventions without being reminded!\r\n\r\nManage memories at [github.com/settings/copilot](https://github.com/settings/copilot).\r\n\r\n❌ Never store secrets!\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Bootstrap all your conventions in one session at project start. 🎯"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Visualize with Mermaid Diagrams",
      "description": "# 🆕 Mermaid Diagrams in Chat (v1.109)\r\n\r\nAsk Copilot:\r\n```\r\nCreate a Mermaid flowchart showing:\r\nAlert Generator → Severity Classifier → Status Manager → Console UI\r\nInclude decision nodes for severity routing.\r\n```\r\n\r\n🔍 **Alt + Scroll** to zoom | 🖱️ **Alt + Drag** to pan | 🖼️ Open in editor\r\n\r\nAlso try:\r\n- \"Create a sequence diagram of alert flow\"\r\n- \"Show alert lifecycle as a state diagram\"\r\n\r\nMermaid code is plain text — paste into GitHub READMEs, Notion, or Confluence.\r\n\r\n**Pro Tip**: Start broad, then refine. Copilot iterates on diagrams like code! 🚀"
    },
    {
      "title": "🆕 Plugins & Marketplace — Share Your Copilot Stack",
      "description": "So far you've customized Copilot with **custom agents**, **skills**, and **prompts** — but only for yourself or this project.\r\n\r\n💡 **What if your whole team could install the same agents and skills with a single command?**\r\n\r\nThat's exactly what the **Copilot CLI Plugin Marketplace** enables:\r\n\r\n- 📦 **Plugin** = a bundled package of agents + skills + MCP configs\r\n- 🏪 **Marketplace** = a Git repo that lists installable plugins\r\n- 🔗 **Distribution** = anyone with repo access installs with one command\r\n\r\nThis is how teams share Copilot customizations **internally** — no manual file copying, no documentation to follow, just one install command.\r\n\r\nLet's walk through how this repo is set up as a marketplace 👇"
    },
    {
      "file": "../.github/plugin/marketplace.json",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "The Marketplace Manifest",
      "description": "The heart of any plugin marketplace is a single file: **`.github/plugin/marketplace.json`**\r\n\r\nCopilot CLI looks for this file when you register a repo as a marketplace. It lists every plugin available to install, along with metadata for browsing.\r\n\r\nThis repo's marketplace (`ghcp-lab-marketplace`) lists 3 plugins:\r\n- `alert-toolkit` — alert analysis agent + triage skill\r\n- `code-reviewer` — code review agent + checklist skill\r\n- `learning-companion` — tutor agent + practice exercise skill\r\n\r\nEach `source` field is the path to the plugin directory, relative to the repo root.\r\n\r\n�� **Team tip**: Host this file in your org's internal tooling repo so everyone can browse and install the same curated agent set."
    },
    {
      "file": "../plugins/alert-toolkit/plugin.json",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Inside a Plugin",
      "description": "Each plugin is a directory with a **`plugin.json`** manifest at its root.\r\n\r\nThis tells Copilot CLI:\r\n- The plugin's name and version\r\n- Where to find its **agents** (`.agent.md` files)\r\n- Where to find its **skills** (`SKILL.md` files)\r\n\r\nThe structure is identical to your project's `.github/agents/` and `.github/skills/` — the only difference is it's **packaged and installable**.\r\n\r\n🎯 Anything you can put in `.github/agents/` or `.github/skills/`, you can put in a plugin and share across your team."
    },
    {
      "file": "../plugins/alert-toolkit/agents/alert-analyst.agent.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Bundling Agents into a Plugin",
      "description": "Agent files inside a plugin are **identical** to the agent files you've seen throughout this lab — same `.agent.md` format, same YAML frontmatter.\r\n\r\nOnce the plugin is installed, this agent is available in every Copilot CLI session automatically:\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin install alert-toolkit@ghcp-lab-marketplace\r\n```\r\n\r\nThen just use it:\r\n```\r\nAnalyze these alerts for anomaly patterns\r\n```\r\n\r\n⚡ **Precedence**: Project-level agents (`.github/agents/`) always win over plugin agents with the same name — so teams can always override a shared plugin locally."
    },
    {
      "file": "../plugins/alert-toolkit/skills/triage-alerts/SKILL.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Bundling Skills into a Plugin",
      "description": "Skills inside a plugin use the same `SKILL.md` format and are invoked the same way — with `/skill-name` in a session.\r\n\r\nAfter installing `alert-toolkit`, the skill is immediately available:\r\n```\r\n/triage-alerts from recent incidents\r\n```\r\n\r\n📦 **What a plugin can bundle:**\r\n- **Agents** — domain experts, workflow automation\r\n- **Skills** — slash commands for repeatable tasks\r\n- **MCP server configs** — connect to APIs, databases, tools\r\n- **Hooks** — auto-run actions on events\r\n\r\nSee `plugins/` at the repo root for all 3 example plugins in this lab."
    },
    {
      "title": "⚠️ Registering a Marketplace",
      "description": "⚠️ **Important**: Marketplace registration runs in a **regular terminal — NOT inside a Copilot CLI session**.\r\n\r\nOpen PowerShell / bash outside the CLI, then:\r\n\r\n**1 — Register the marketplace:**\r\n```sh\r\n# From a GitHub repo\r\ncopilot plugin marketplace add OWNER/ghcp-lab\r\n\r\n# Or from a local clone (use an absolute path)\r\ncopilot plugin marketplace add C:\\path\\to\\ghcp-lab\r\n```\r\n\r\n**2 — Browse available plugins:**\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin marketplace browse ghcp-lab-marketplace\r\n```\r\n\r\n**3 — Install a plugin:**\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin install alert-toolkit@ghcp-lab-marketplace\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou only register once. After that, every plugin in that marketplace is always available to install."
    },
    {
      "title": "Using Installed Plugins",
      "description": "Once installed, plugin agents and skills appear alongside your project's own customizations — automatically.\r\n\r\nVerify installation (outside CLI):\r\n```sh\r\ncopilot plugin list\r\n```\r\n\r\nInside a Copilot CLI session, confirm they loaded:\r\n```\r\n/agent\r\n/skills list\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Then use them naturally in any session:**\r\n```\r\nAnalyze these alerts for patterns\r\n/triage-alerts from the last 24 hours\r\n```\r\n\r\n🏢 **Ship this to your team in 3 steps:**\r\n1. Create a shared repo with your agents, skills, and `marketplace.json`\r\n2. Share one command: `copilot plugin marketplace add YOUR-ORG/copilot-tools`\r\n3. Everyone installs the plugins they need — same tools, consistent behavior\r\n\r\n📖 [Copilot CLI Plugin Reference](https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/reference/cli-plugin-reference)"
    },
